VALUES ('O’Brien commends ‘Changing Ireland’ for “track record” of positivity and critical journalism', 'obrien-commends-changing-ireland-for-track-record-of-positivity-and-critical-journalism', '<p>He said:</p>
<blockquote><p>“Congratulations to chairperson Shane O’Sullivan and his board and staff for managing and supporting an unique project now publishing for 21 years. It is all the more important today to have trustworthy media,” <strong>said Minister O’Brien.</strong></p></blockquote>
<p>He added:</p>
<blockquote><p>“‘Changing Ireland’ has a track record in promoting community development, giving marginalised communities and people who are hard to reach a voice, and highlighting positive local and national initiatives. It is also at times critical and that is to be welcomed.”</p></blockquote>
<p>The minister was welcomed to Moyross by <strong>Shane O’Sullivan</strong>, chairperson of Changing Ireland Community Media CLG, the not-for-profit company that publishes the magazine with support from the minister’s Department and with a small amount of funding earned from social enterprise activities.</p>
<p>The minister was shown the bungalow in Sarsfield Gardens where the magazine was based for its first 17 years before it moved into new offices in Moyross Community Hub.</p>
<blockquote><p>“Being part of the community has always been core to the ‘Changing Ireland’ project,” said Mr. O’Sullivan. “The project is rooted in Moyross and gives as well as receives support locally.”</p></blockquote>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/3-mag-covers-Moyross-Pres-Higgins-Philly-McMahon.png" alt="" width="1705" height="805" srcset="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/3-mag-covers-Moyross-Pres-Higgins-Philly-McMahon.png 1705w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/3-mag-covers-Moyross-Pres-Higgins-Philly-McMahon-300x142.png 300w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/3-mag-covers-Moyross-Pres-Higgins-Philly-McMahon-1024x483.png 1024w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/3-mag-covers-Moyross-Pres-Higgins-Philly-McMahon-768x363.png 768w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/3-mag-covers-Moyross-Pres-Higgins-Philly-McMahon-1536x725.png 1536w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/3-mag-covers-Moyross-Pres-Higgins-Philly-McMahon-1080x510.png 1080w" sizes="(max-width: 1705px) 100vw, 1705px" /></p>
<p>The magazine employs one person as editor/manager and engages reporters on a freelance basis.</p>
<blockquote><p>“Our work is underpinned by Community development principles and the company is managed by a voluntary board. Now, Changing Ireland is the most popular and longest-established Community and Voluntary Sector magazine published in Ireland. It is a trusted and valued news source,” said Mr. O’Sullivan.</p></blockquote>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Minister-of-State-Joe-OBrien-visited-Changing-Ireland-this-week-as-the-magazine-celebrated-its-21st-birthday-scaled.jpg" alt="" width="2560" height="1519" srcset="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Minister-of-State-Joe-OBrien-visited-Changing-Ireland-this-week-as-the-magazine-celebrated-its-21st-birthday-scaled.jpg 2560w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Minister-of-State-Joe-OBrien-visited-Changing-Ireland-this-week-as-the-magazine-celebrated-its-21st-birthday-300x178.jpg 300w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Minister-of-State-Joe-OBrien-visited-Changing-Ireland-this-week-as-the-magazine-celebrated-its-21st-birthday-1024x607.jpg 1024w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Minister-of-State-Joe-OBrien-visited-Changing-Ireland-this-week-as-the-magazine-celebrated-its-21st-birthday-768x456.jpg 768w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Minister-of-State-Joe-OBrien-visited-Changing-Ireland-this-week-as-the-magazine-celebrated-its-21st-birthday-1536x911.jpg 1536w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Minister-of-State-Joe-OBrien-visited-Changing-Ireland-this-week-as-the-magazine-celebrated-its-21st-birthday-2048x1215.jpg 2048w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Minister-of-State-Joe-OBrien-visited-Changing-Ireland-this-week-as-the-magazine-celebrated-its-21st-birthday-1080x641.jpg 1080w" sizes="(max-width: 2560px) 100vw, 2560px" /></p>
<p><strong>ABOVE: </strong><em>Minister of State Joe O&#8217;Brien visited Changing Ireland on Monday as the magazine celebrated its 21st birthday. Pictured are: Board members Andrew O’Byrne and George Clancy, editor Allen Meagher, student on placement Caoimhe Lalor, founding member Viv Sadd, Minister Joe O’Brien and journalist Kathy Masterson.</em></p>
<p>Editor <strong>Allen Meagher</strong> said,</p>
<blockquote><p>“The magazine was first spoken of in 1999 and two years later a community project in Moyross won the national contract and ‘Changing Ireland’ was born. Constant negative headlines and stigmatisation which Moyross people face prompted local activists at the time to get behind the project. People need to hear of the positive initiatives happening nationally in communities around Ireland – including Moyross.”</p></blockquote>
<p>He thanked everyone behind the venture including community workers nationally, activists in Moyross since the beginning, the Department in its various iterations through the years, current and past board members, editorial team members, reporters and readers.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Covers-with-Mary-Coughlan-Seán-Óg-Ó-hAilpín-and-Bernadette-McAliskey.png" alt="" width="1865" height="874" srcset="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Covers-with-Mary-Coughlan-Seán-Óg-Ó-hAilpín-and-Bernadette-McAliskey.png 1865w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Covers-with-Mary-Coughlan-Seán-Óg-Ó-hAilpín-and-Bernadette-McAliskey-300x141.png 300w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Covers-with-Mary-Coughlan-Seán-Óg-Ó-hAilpín-and-Bernadette-McAliskey-1024x480.png 1024w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Covers-with-Mary-Coughlan-Seán-Óg-Ó-hAilpín-and-Bernadette-McAliskey-768x360.png 768w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Covers-with-Mary-Coughlan-Seán-Óg-Ó-hAilpín-and-Bernadette-McAliskey-1536x720.png 1536w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Covers-with-Mary-Coughlan-Seán-Óg-Ó-hAilpín-and-Bernadette-McAliskey-1080x506.png 1080w" sizes="(max-width: 1865px) 100vw, 1865px" /></p>
<p>Among those to have featured in the magazine over the years are <strong>Mary Coughlan, Seán Óg Ó hAilpín</strong> and <strong>Bernadette McAliskey</strong>. However, most covers feature ordinary folk doing extraordinary things in communities. The <strong>first edition</strong> featured a nun, <a href="https://www.limerickpost.ie/2022/03/07/life-and-work-of-limerick-nun-documented-in-new-album/">Sr. Mary Carmel</a>, from Thomondgate, Limerick, disguised as a man as part of a ‘Challenging Stereotypes’ project.</p>
<p><a href="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2020/11/Issue-1-28p-Summer-2001-readable.pdf"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Issue-1-cover-1.png" alt="" width="662" height="945" srcset="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Issue-1-cover-1.png 662w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Issue-1-cover-1-210x300.png 210w" sizes="(max-width: 662px) 100vw, 662px" /></a></p>
<p>By year’s end, Changing Ireland Community Media CLG is due to complete a strategic plan for the coming years – with part of that vision being to increase the level of news output and improve the publication’s online presence and appeal.</p>
<p><strong>Website</strong>: <a href="https://changingireland.ie/">https://changingireland.ie/</a></p>
<figure aria-describedby="caption-attachment-252298"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/DSCF0109.jpg" alt="" width="2048" height="1536" srcset="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/DSCF0109.jpg 2048w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/DSCF0109-300x225.jpg 300w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/DSCF0109-1024x768.jpg 1024w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/DSCF0109-768x576.jpg 768w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/DSCF0109-1536x1152.jpg 1536w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/DSCF0109-1080x810.jpg 1080w" sizes="(max-width: 2048px) 100vw, 2048px" /><figcaption>• Sarsfield Gardens, Moyross: From 2001 to 2017, &#8216;Changing Ireland&#8217; was based in a refurbished bungalow, a locally-led early example of regeneration.</figcaption></figure>
<p><strong>Call to visit us &#8211;</strong> just ask at reception or knock on the door or better still email or ring in advance. We are now based in Moyross Community Hub, pictured below.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Moyross-Community-Hub-2.jpg" alt="" width="960" height="719" srcset="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Moyross-Community-Hub-2.jpg 960w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Moyross-Community-Hub-2-300x225.jpg 300w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Moyross-Community-Hub-2-768x575.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 960px) 100vw, 960px" /></p>
<blockquote><p><a href="https://changingireland.ie/what-we-love-about-moyross/">What we love about Moyross</a></p></blockquote>
<p><iframe sandbox="allow-scripts" security="restricted" title="“What we love about Moyross” — Changing Ireland" src="https://changingireland.ie/what-we-love-about-moyross/embed/#?secret=AIiuRVloW5#?secret=iKk50nZFen" width="600" height="338" frameborder="0" marginwidth="0" marginheight="0" scrolling="no"></iframe></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>', '‘Changing Ireland’ welcomed Minister of State for Community Development, Joe O’Brien, to Moyross this week as our publication celebrates 21 years publishing news from communities around the country. Over the years, the magazine has featured community initiatives, volunteers and activists from every county – from Erris Head to Wexford town, from Dublin to Derry and Kerry – and the minister commended the magazine for its coverage and its “track record” and for being “at times critical”.', 'https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Proudly-publishing-from-Moyross-for-21-years-1.png', '', '[{"slug":"community","title":"Community"}]', '[{"slug":"changingireland","title":"#ChangingIreland"},{"slug":"changing-ireland","title":"Changing Ireland"},{"slug":"community-development","title":"community development"},{"slug":"department-rural-community-development-drcd","title":"Department of Rural and Community Development"},{"slug":"minister-joe-obrien","title":"Minister Joe O''Brien"},{"slug":"moyross","title":"Moyross"}]', '', 252292, 1, '2022-10-11T18:11:18Z', '2022-10-11T19:13:40Z', '2022-10-11T18:11:18Z')
